<MainLayout title="Drain Problems & Sewer Clogs | Mason City Iowa" description="Answers to common drain and sewer problems in Mason City Iowa: basement drain backups, multiple slow drains, tree roots, recurring clogs and main sewer backups."><section class="hero"><div class="wrap"><p class="eyebrow">Real drain problems</p><h1>What Is Causing the Backup?</h1><p>The pattern of a clog often tells where to look. One slow sink is different from three drains backing up at once.</p></div></section><section class="section"><div class="wrap faq"><details open><summary>Why is the basement floor drain backing up?</summary><p>Because it is low in the drainage system, it may show a blockage farther down the line. If wastewater rises there when another fixture runs, the main drain or sewer line may be involved.</p></details><details><summary>Why are multiple drains backing up at once?</summary><p>When a toilet, shower and floor drain are affected together, the blockage is more likely in a shared drain or main sewer line.</p></details><details><summary>Why does the sewer keep clogging after it was cleaned?</summary><p>Recurring blockage can come from tree roots, buildup, damaged pipe or another restriction inside the line.</p></details><details><summary>Can tree roots grow into a sewer pipe?</summary><p>Yes. Roots can enter through cracks and joints in buried sewer pipe and create a recurring restriction.</p></details><details><summary>What causes a sewer smell in the basement?</summary><p>A dry trap, drain problem or sewer backup can produce an odor. The source matters, especially when odor is paired with slow drainage or wastewater backup.</p></details></div></section></MainLayout>